    "https://illinois.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/18084?c=illinois": {
      "title": "Asst/Assoc Professor, Sustainable Change - Department of Recreation, Sport, & Tourism",
      "description": "Asst/Assoc Professor, Sustainable Change - Department of Recreation, Sport, & Tourism | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Asst/Assoc Professor, Sustainable Change - Department of Recreation, Sport, & Tourism Urbana, IL, United States | 1038549 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search Assistant/Associate Professor, Sustainable Change in Recreation or Tourism Department of Recreation, Sport and Tourism College of Applied Health Sciences University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign The Department of Recreation, Sport and Tourism (RST) at the University of Illinois\nUrbana-Champaign invites applications for a nine-month (paid over 12 months),\ntenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position in Sustainable\nChange in Recreation or Tourism, with a preference for environmental and/or\nnature-based scholarship. We seek candidates with a strong, well-defined\nresearch agenda and the ability to teach recreation or tourism courses, as well\nas other courses in the RST core curriculum. This position builds on the\nDepartment’s interdisciplinary strengths in community and organizational\ndevelopment, individual health and wellbeing, sustainable change, and\ndiversity, equity, and inclusion. The start date for this position will be\nAugust 16, 2027. Responsibilities Responsibilities include: Conducting research with a focus on sustainable change in aspects of recreation or tourism, with a preference for environmental and/or nature-based scholarship. Building collaborative research partnerships with colleagues within the Department, College, and various disciplines across campus. Advising and directing student research, including theses and dissertations. Instructing undergraduate and graduate courses. Contributing to the Department's curriculum. Seeking external funding for research efforts. Serving on Department, College, and University committees. Engaging in professional and public service activities. Minimum Qualifications To qualify for the position, applicants must demonstrate the following minimum qualifications: Earned doctorate in recreation, tourism, or an affiliated field at the time of appointment. Applicants with ABD status will be considered, but the candidate must complete their doctoral degree prior to the position’s start date. Evidence of or potential to publish in top-tier peer-reviewed journals. Evidence of or potential to secure external funding to support one’s research program. Ability to teach undergraduate and graduate courses within the RST department while demonstrating ability to make linkages among recreation, sport, and tourism. Ability to mentor doctoral and master’s students. Ability to contribute to the University and the Recreation, Sport and Tourism field through academic service and public engagement. The Department: Faculty and staff of the Department of Recreation, Sport and\nTourism (RST) at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign work to advance knowledge to foster healthy\nlifestyles, environmental stewardship and sustainability, effective management and marketing of organizations, and social\nand economic development of communities (see www.rst.illinois.edufor additional information). The department offers residential B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs, and an online M.S. program. It also houses the Office of Park\nand Recreation Resources. The College: The RST\nDepartment is part of the College of Applied\nHealth Sciences (see http://ahs.illinois.edu) and\naffiliated with the Center for Health, Aging,\nand Disability (see https://ahs.illinois.edu/research-and-community/center-on-health-aging-and- disability). The College houses the Division of Disability Resources and Educational\nServices (http://www.disability.illinois.edu) (DRES),\nwhich includes the U.S. Paralympic Training Site for Wheelchair Track Racing. The College also houses the Chez Veterans Center (https://ahs.illinois.edu/chez-veterans-center), which\nopened in 2015 to provide support services to empower student veterans, military-connected members, and their\nfamilies. The Community: Urbana-Champaign\n(located 135 miles south of Chicago) combines natural Midwestern beauty with\nthe cultural resources typically found in larger metropolitan areas. Three\ndowntown areas (Urbana, Champaign, and Campustown) provide vibrant public spaces with unique dining and entertainment options and\nthe local community offers a thriving arts and culture atmosphere as well as\nvarsity and community sports. Urbana-Champaign was rated #1 by Midwest Living\nmagazine for the greatest food for a city with less than 750,000 population.\nOutdoor enthusiasts can enjoy 80 parks within the two communities with easy\naccess to several large forest preserves and the Kickapoo Rail Trails in Champaign County (see www.visitchampaigncounty.orgfor additional information). Appointment Information This is a 100% full-time Academic position, with a 9-month service basis, paid over 12-months. The expected start date\nis 8/16/2027. The budgeted salary range for the position is $75,000 to\n$95,000 at the Assistant Professor level, $85,000 to $110,000 at\nthe Associate Professor level. Final\nsalary offered will be determined by a thorough assessment of available market\ndata, internal salary equity, candidate experience and qualifications,\ncollective bargaining agreements, and budget constraints. This position is eligible for sponsorship for work authorization, except for\nsponsorship of a new H1B petition that would incur the $100,000 fee. Application Procedures & Deadline Information For full consideration, please submit all required materials by 6:00 pm (Central Time) on November 13, 2026. Apply for this position using the Apply Now button at the top or bottom of this posting. Applications not submitted through https://jobs.illinois.edu will not be considered. Applications\nmaterials should include the following: (1) your letter of application that\nincludes a statement of your research, teaching, and service and explains how\nyou meet the qualifications outlined above,(2) your curriculum vitae, and (3) names\nand contact information for three references. Candidates will be notified\nbefore references are contacted and letters requested. For further information about this specific\nposition, please contact Dr. Mariela\nFernandez, Search Committee Chair, mfrnndz2@illinois.edu,\n(217) 333-7084, or ahs-hr@illinois.edu. For questions regarding the application process, please contact 217-333-2137. Founded in 1867 as Illinois’s flagship public university, the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign empowers a global community of students, scholars, and alumni to change the world through bold, innovative research and transformative education. With a land-grant heritage rooted in progress, Illinois faculty drive paradigm-shifting discoveries, mentor future leaders, and collaborate across 15 colleges and instructional units and more than 20 research institutes. The university’s legacy of excellence includes 24 Nobel Prizes and 29 Pulitzer Prizes awarded to its faculty and alumni, $755 million in sponsored research funding, and 94 academic programs ranked in the top 20 nationally. Illinois offers the resources, recognition, and reach to accelerate your career — along with a highly competitive benefits portfolio. Discover how the Illinois Value Proposition fosters professional longevity, personal fulfillment, and a deep sense of purpose in every role. Faculty Recruitment Champaign-Urbana Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position; however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify . Applicants with disabilities are encouraged to apply and may request a reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act (2008) to complete the application and/or interview process. Accommodations may also be requested on the basis of pregnancy, childbirth, and related conditions, or religion. Requests may be submitted through the reasonable accommodation portal , or by contacting the Office for Access & Equity at 217-333-0885, option #1, or accessibility@illinois.edu . Requisition ID : 1038549 Job Category : Faculty Apply at: https://jobs.illinois.edu Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois Positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois' locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Chicago University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation Illinois Human Resources University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign Personnel Services Building - MC 562 52 E. Gregory Dr. Champaign, IL  61820 217.333.2137 | jobs@illinois.edu humanresources.illinois.edu"
    },
    "https://illinois.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/18184?c=illinois": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor, Computer Animation",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor, Computer Animation | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor, Computer Animation Urbana, IL, United States | 1038723 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/12/2026 Back to Search Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant\nProfessor, Computer Animation School of Art & Design, Studio Art Program College of Fine & Applied Arts University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign The School of Art & Design (A&D) at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-system Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor in Computer Animation within the Studio Art Program. We seek an accomplished artist-educator and/or creative practitioner whose work engages animation as a form of artistic expression, storytelling, image-making, and contemporary studio practice. This position offers an opportunity to contribute to and expand animation-related teaching and creative practice within an interdisciplinary Studio Art program. We are looking for a versatile practitioner who brings substantial expertise in animation while engaging meaningfully with the broader artistic, technological, and cultural contexts of the School. The successful candidate will contribute to student interests in animation while developing curricular connections between animation and drawing, illustration, painting, 3D practice, interaction, game development, and other forms of contemporary studio art. Animation at Illinois is situated within a broad studio-art environment rather than a standalone animation production program. We are particularly interested in candidates who understand animation as both a technical and artistic practice that can intersect with drawing, sequential art, illustration, painting, digital image-making, 3D practice, interactive media, computational art, games, and other forms of visual and time-based practice. The position's primary home will be the Studio Art Program in the School of Art & Design. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to collaborate with colleagues in the School of Information Sciences through the interdisciplinary Game Studies & Design programs, including the Master of Science in Game Development and undergraduate Game Studies & Design minor. These connections offer students additional avenues for exploring animation in interactive and game-related contexts while maintaining the position's clear curricular and intellectual home in Studio Art. About the College of Fine and Applied Arts The College of Fine and Applied Arts (FAA) is singular in the nation for its diversity, innovation and breadth. Its mission is dedicated to the advancement, practice, and understanding of the arts, and its central focus is the synergy between research and the preparation of students for professional careers in the creation and interpretation of the environmental, visual, and performing arts. Deeply related to that focus is the commitment to elevate and sustain the study of the arts as both a necessary mode of understanding and a vibrant expression of human experience within the local, national, and international communities. The college comprises the Schools of Music , Architecture , and Art & Design ; Departments of Dance , Landscape Architecture , Theatre , and Urban and Regional Planning ; Sustainable Design : Krannert Center for the Performing Arts ; Krannert Art Museum; and Japan House . FAA employs more than 660 faculty members and staff, including more than 150 tenure system faculty members, and more than 1,850 undergraduate and 635 graduate students enroll in FAA’s more than 30 majors and 10 minors. About the School of Art & Design Located within the College of Fine and Applied Arts, A&D is home to a diverse and vibrant community of artists, designers, educators, and historians. We support interdisciplinary and socially engaged creative practices and offer a range of undergraduate and graduate degree programs. Our Studio Art program emphasizes both technical excellence and conceptual rigor, encouraging students to explore the intersections of art, technology, and culture. The School's interdisciplinary environment provides opportunities for artists working across media and disciplines. Existing areas of Studio Art include illustration, painting, photo, 3D practice, interaction and critical technologies, fashion, printmaking, and performance, creating a rich context in which animation-related teaching and creative practices can intersect with and contribute to established studio disciplines. For more about the School of Art & Design and its programs, visit art.illinois.edu . About the Position This search welcomes candidates whose preparation comes through different professional and educational pathways. Candidates with terminal degrees or equivalent expertise and experience, along with demonstrated records or potential for strong instructional contributions, may be considered for appointment as Teaching Assistant Professor . Candidates whose qualifications are grounded in substantial professional practice and specialized expertise may be considered for appointment as Clinical Assistant Professor . Both are full-time specialized faculty appointments contributing to the teaching mission of the School. The successful candidate will contribute to animation-related teaching and creative practice within Studio Art at Illinois, supporting current offerings and exploring opportunities for future curricular development. This includes teaching existing interdisciplinary studio courses, contributing to animation-related curriculum and course development as appropriate, mentoring students across levels of experience, and contributing to the intellectual and creative life of the School. We are seeking an educator who can balance technical and production knowledge with artistic development, conceptual thinking, critical inquiry, and experimentation. The successful candidate should be comfortable working with students who enter animation from different disciplinary backgrounds and with varying levels of technical experience. Areas of Expertise We welcome candidates working across a broad range of animation practices, including but not limited to: · 2D animation, including traditional, digital, and hybrid approaches · 3D animation, modeling, rendering, rigging, and related practices · Character, environment, and visual development · Motion graphics and time-based media · Experimental, independent, and expanded forms of animation · Visual effects and compositing · Storyboarding, sequential art, and visual narrative · Animation for games, film, television, VFX, or other media · Interactive and game-engine-based animation · Procedural, generative, or computational approaches to animation · Emerging forms of digital and computational image-making Candidates need not demonstrate expertise in all of these areas. We are particularly interested in candidates who demonstrate depth in animation production alongside the adaptability and breadth needed to work across an interdisciplinary studio-art environment. Experience with software and platforms such as Adobe Creative Cloud, Blender, Maya, Cinema 4D, Unity, Unreal Engine, or comparable tools is welcome. We also welcome candidates whose practices engage emerging technologies and evolving forms of digital image-making. Teaching and Interdisciplinary Collaboration The successful candidate's primary appointment will be within the Studio Art Program in A&D. They will contribute to animation-related curriculum while participating fully in the broader teaching and intellectual life of the interdisciplinary Studio Art program. Studio Art already provides strong opportunities for students interested in animation to develop related practices in areas such as illustration, drawing, sequential art, painting, 3D practice, interaction, printmaking, and time based media. The successful candidate will build on these existing strengths rather than duplicate them. The candidate will also have opportunities to collaborate with colleagues in the School of Information Sciences through Game Studies & Design, including the Master of Science in Game Development and undergraduate Game Studies & Design minor. These connections may provide additional opportunities for students interested in animation, games, interactive storytelling, and related technologies. Potential areas of interdisciplinary connection include: Illustration and sequential art Drawing and digital image-making Painting and mixed media 3D modeling and imaging Interaction and interactive media Time-based practices Computational and generative art Game development and interactive storytelling Narrative and world-building Emerging technologies and visual culture The successful candidate may propose or develop new courses in animation and related areas as curricular needs and resources permit. Depending on expertise and program needs, teaching may also include existing courses in illustration, drawing, digital image-making, interaction, time-based media, interdisciplinary studio and seminar courses, and graduate studio. Qualifications for Teaching Assistant Professor Required Qualifications Terminal degree or equivalent expertise and experience in animation, studio art, illustration, digital media, game development, computational media, or a related field Demonstrated professional, artistic, or creative practice in animation or a closely related field Demonstrated experience teaching or mentoring students in academic and/or professional settings Demonstrated expertise in at least one major area of animation production, such as 2D animation, 3D animation, motion graphics, visual effects, or related time-based media Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated depth in 2D and/or 3D animation with the ability to teach animation production at the undergraduate level Professional or production experience working through animation workflows that extend into post-production, including compositing, editing, visual effects, rendering, or related processes Technical fluency with contemporary animation and digital production tools and workflows Experience connecting animation practice to broader artistic, conceptual, cultural, technological, or critical contexts Ability to teach and mentor students within an interdisciplinary studio-art environment Experience working across animation and related areas such as illustration, 3D practice, interactive media, games, or emerging technologies Qualifications for Clinical Assistant Professor Required Qualifications · Substantial professional experience in animation, digital media, games, visual effects, film, television, or a related creative field · Demonstrated professional expertise in at least one major area of animation production, such as 2D animation, 3D animation, motion graphics, visual effects, or related time-based media · Demonstrated technical fluency with relevant animation, digital, and/or post-production workflows Preferred Qualifications Professional production experience in 2D and/or 3D animation, particularly within studio, commercial, independent, freelance, or other collaborative production environments Experience with animation post-production processes, including compositing, editing, visual effects, rendering, or related workflows Demonstrated experience teaching or mentoring students in academic and/or professional settings Ability to translate professional production expertise into an effective, inclusive, and exploratory educational environment Experience connecting professional animation practice to related areas such as illustration, 3D practice, interactive media, games, or emerging technologies Demonstrated ability to situate professional and creative practice within broader artistic, cultural, technological, and critical contexts Professional experience may include work in commercial studios, independent practice, freelance or commissioned production, games, film, television, VFX, interactive media, emerging media, or other relevant creative fields. Creative and Pedagogical Perspectives We welcome applicants whose creative and pedagogical practices expand or challenge dominant narratives in animation and related media. This may include work that engages underrepresented histories, global perspectives, emergent media, experimental practices, or innovative modes of storytelling and image-making. We are particularly interested in candidates whose teaching and creative practices demonstrate sensitivity to a wide range of cultural frameworks, lived experiences, and ways of knowing. We seek educators who can help students develop not only technical proficiency, but also distinctive artistic voices, conceptual frameworks, critical perspectives, and approaches to making. Candidates should demonstrate an ability to create learning environments in which students can experiment, take creative risks, engage in critique, and understand animation within broader contexts of art, culture, technology, and contemporary life. Application Materials Required application materials include: Cover letter Please describe your creative and teaching practices and how they could contribute to animation-related teaching and creative practice within an interdisciplinary Studio Art program. Please discuss potential connections between your practice and areas such as illustration, painting, 3D practice, interaction, computational media, and/or game studies. Candidates may also use the cover letter to describe how their professional, artistic, or educational background prepares them for either the Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor pathway. Curriculum vitae Teaching statement Portfolio of creative work Please submit materials that provide a clear representation of your creative practice and its range. Applicants may submit a PDF portfolio, a link to an online portfolio, a demo reel, or a combination of these formats. For applicants working in animation and other time-based media, a demo reel is encouraged. Portfolios may include finished works as well as relevant process materials such as storyboards, animation stills, visual development, or other examples that help contextualize the work. Please identify your role in collaborative or professionally produced work when appropriate. Samples of student work , if available Names and contact information for three references Appointment Information This is a 100% full-time, nine-month Teaching Assistant Professor or Clinical Assistant Professor appointment (paid over 12 months), with an initial one-year appointment that may be renewed subject to programmatic needs and available resources. The expected start date is August 16, 2027. The salary range for the position is $65,000–$70,000 . We strive to provide a competitive salary, considering factors such as available market data, internal equity, candidate’s experience and qualifications, and budget constraints. This position is eligible for sponsorship except for sponsorship of a new H1B petition that would incur the $100,000 fee. Application Procedures & Deadline Information Applications must be received by 6:00pm (Central Time) on December 15, 2026 . Apply for this position using the Apply Now button at the top or bottom of this posting. Applications not submitted through jobs.illinois.edu will not be considered. For further information about this specific position, please contact Bobbie Clegg at bjclegg@illinois.edu . For questions regarding the application process, please contact 217-333-2137 . Founded in 1867 as Illinois’s flagship public university, the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign empowers a global community of students, scholars, and alumni to change the world through bold, innovative research and transformative education. With a land-grant heritage rooted in progress, Illinois faculty drive paradigm-shifting discoveries, mentor future leaders, and collaborate across 15 colleges and instructional units and more than 20 research institutes. The university’s legacy of excellence includes 24 Nobel Prizes and 29 Pulitzer Prizes awarded to its faculty and alumni, $755 million in sponsored research funding, and 94 academic programs ranked in the top 20 nationally. Illinois offers the resources, recognition, and reach to accelerate your career — along with a highly competitive benefits portfolio. Discover how the Illinois Value Proposition fosters professional longevity, personal fulfillment, and a deep sense of purpose in every role. Faculty Recruitment Champaign-Urbana Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position; however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify . Applicants with disabilities are encouraged to apply and may request a reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act (2008) to complete the application and/or interview process. Accommodations may also be requested on the basis of pregnancy, childbirth, and related conditions, or religion. Requests may be submitted through the reasonable accommodation portal , or by contacting the Office for Access & Equity at 217-333-0885, option #1, or accessibility@illinois.edu . Requisition ID : 1038723 Job Category : Faculty Apply at: https://jobs.illinois.edu Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois Positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois' locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Chicago University of Illinois Springfield University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation Illinois Human Resources University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign Personnel Services Building - MC 562 52 E. Gregory Dr. Champaign, IL  61820 217.333.2137 | jobs@illinois.edu humanresources.illinois.edu"
    },
